\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {1}\textcolor {blue}{Sketches of the System: In Paper}}{3}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Depicted is the $yz$-projection of the incline system, the $z$-direction is orthogonal to the paper plane. The incline has an inclination angle $\varphi $ and the sphere is of radius $R$. Two orthonormal coordinate frames are considered: The fixed frame $\{ \bm  {e}_x, \bm  {e}_y, \bm  {e}_z\}$ and the body frame $\{ \hat  {\bm  {e}}_x, \hat  {\bm  {e}}_y, \hat  {\bm  {e}}_z \}$. Vectors expressed in the body frame carry hats and are time-dependent, stationary vectors not. Blue vectors might have a non-zero $z$-component and are therefore only projections. The outer magnetic field is represented by $\bm  {B}$, the magnetic dipole vector of the sphere by $\hat  {\bm  {\mu }}(t)$, and the centre of mass measured from the fixed frame by $\bm  {r}(t)$. The in-plane angular velocities of the body frame are shown as $\hat  {\bm  {\omega }}_x$ and $\hat  {\bm  {\omega }}_y$.}}{3}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:ramp}{{1}{3}{}{figure.1}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Shown is a sketch of the flat system with sufficiently small initial displacement angle $\beta _0$ between the outer magnetic field $\bm  {B}$ and the initial magnetic dipole vector $\bm  {\mu }_0$. Note that the system is effectively reduced to two dimensions. The red vectors indicate the oscillation-driving electromagnetic force $\bm  {F}_\text  {em}$ and the damping frictional force $\bm  {F}_\text  {fric}$. The frictional force includes aerodynamical drag and rolling resistance.}}{4}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:flat}{{2}{4}{}{figure.2}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Parametrizations of $\bm  {\mu }_0$ in F and G.}}{4}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {2}\textcolor {blue}{Figure Family A: Appendix}}{5}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {A1}{\ignorespaces Shown are different evolutions of the $y$-projection of the centre-of-mass coordinate $\bm  {r}=(x,y,z)^\top $ over time $t$ for different incline angles $\varphi $ without friction. Lines were calculated from the analytical solution and compared to the numerical results, represented as dots.}}{5}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {A2/A3}{\ignorespaces The left plot shows the energy evolution $E(t)$ of the system over time $t$, while the right one depicts the $y$-COM-coordinate evolution. The data originates from the numerical simulation program. Compared are different friction models, where \emph  {Coulomb} represents dry rolling resistance, \emph  {viscous} represents a linear damping approximation to rolling resistance, and \emph  {aerodynamic} represents spherical aerodynamic drag.}}{6}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {3}Figure Family B}{7}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {4}Figure Family C}{8}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {5}\textcolor {blue}{Figure Family F: In Paper}}{9}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {F1}{\ignorespaces Final $x$-deviation w.r.t. polar angle and modulus. Figure (a) and (b) both show the $x$-value of a trajectory after $t=1 \, \text  {s}$ depending on the initial polar angle. Each graph represents a modulus of $\bm  {\mu }$, indicated by the color bar. The first ten graphs in (a) show that the trajectorial deviation incrases non-linearly with the angular misalignment between $\bm  {\mu }$ and the $yz$-plane. As expected, the deviation is antisymmetric about the origin. For increasing values of $\mu $ and $\varphi $, the deviation saturates. In (b), the curves for the remaining moduli $\mu $ can be seen. In this range, the magnetic forces start to dominate over the gravitational force for high polar deviations. The extrema of the curves correspond to the point where trajectories begin to loop around the origin instead of following the incline.}}{9}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {F2}{\ignorespaces Chosen trajectories for fixed moduli. Figures (a), (b), and (c) show trajectories projected on the $xy$-plane for fixed values of $\mu $ and eleven equally spaced polar angle samples. It can be seen that in all cases, higher angular deviations correspond to stronger deflection of the sphere from a straight path. Increasing the modulus increases the curvature of the trajectories which evolve from almost linear paths to near-circular arcs. Figure (c) also shows the behaviour past the critical points of Figure F1(b) where the outmost paths form closed loops.}}{10}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {6}\textcolor {blue}{Figure Family G: In Paper}}{11}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {G}{\ignorespaces Total $y$-displacement after $t=1 \, \text  {s}$ for different polar angles (a) and associated representative $y$-projected trajectories (b). Figure (a) shows the total distance in $y$ the sphere has rolled after one second depending on the polar misalignment of $\bm  {\mu }$ and $\bm  {B}$. There are three discernible regions: For almost all positive and a small range of negative deviations, the sphere covers distances characteristic for downward rolling motion. In the negative regime and around zero, the sphere is remaining around the origin. Between these regions, there are two steep boundary regions indicating an aprupt change between downward motion and oscillation around the origin. Figure (b) shows the evolution of the $y$-coordinate in time for three representative trajectories as indicated by the points in (a). The non-downward motion corresponds clearly to oscillation around the origin, while the downward motion shows only very weak signs of oscillatory motion in the beginning. The intermediate curve displays strong oscillatory motion in the beginning which is then overcome by gravity.}}{12}{}\protected@file@percent }
